kb is fast on small stores. Its costs grow with document size and store size in a few known places. Measure them first, then fix the ones that matter.

Known from the performance review (2026-09-26):
- embedding runs at about 0.58 s per chunk, so a 300-page PDF takes minutes and can exceed an agent's tool timeout;
- every hybrid search is a new process that maps the model file and embeds the query;
- every add rebuilds the keyword index from the whole store;
- the graph view's force layout compares every pair of nodes at each step, on the webview's main thread;
- vector search compares against every chunk, which is fine to around 100k chunks.

The core, in `packages/coboard`.

**Done when:**
- `Board.archive(id)` / `unarchive(id)` append `{"op":"archive"|"unarchive","id","at"}` under the lock. Archiving an archived item, or unarchiving one that is not archived, is refused with a clear error;
- the fold tracks archive state apart from the item, so a later `put` (even from an older coboard) leaves it unchanged;
- an item is **effectively archived** when it, its milestone or its epic is archived. Nothing is written to the children;
- `all()` and `search()` take an optional `archived: "exclude" | "include" | "only"` (default `exclude`); `get(id)` always works and reports `archived` and, when inherited, which ancestor it comes from;
- archived ids stay reserved: the next id never reuses one;
- creating or moving an item into an archived epic or milestone is refused;
- an older reader given a log with archive records still loads it (the unknown ops are skipped). This is covered by a test;
- tests cover the fold, inheritance, the filter modes, id reservation and the refusals.

The agent-facing side. It adds to the API and changes nothing that exists.

**Done when:**
- `board_list` and `board_search` take an optional `archived` (`exclude` default, `include`, `only`). A call without it behaves exactly as before on a board with nothing archived;
- `board_get` returns `archived` (and the ancestor it is inherited from). An archived epic's view lists its archived children when asked with `archived`;
- new `board_archive` and `board_unarchive` tools, each taking an id and an optional reason that is recorded as a comment on a ticket;
- the server instructions say what archiving is for (finished work out of the way), that it is reversible, and that agents still never delete;
- tests in `mcp` cover the new parameter, the default, and both tools. They depend on the core ticket.

The VS Code side, in `packages/coboard-vscode`.

**Done when:**
- the sidebar's filter has **Include archived**, off by default and kept with the filter state. Archived rows, when shown, are marked (dimmed, with an archive icon);
- right-click (and the item page's menu) has **Archive** / **Unarchive**. Archiving an epic or milestone says how many items it hides;
- an archived item's page shows a banner ("Archived", or "Archived with E-2") with an **Unarchive** button;
- epic and milestone pages and the Kanban view leave archived items out;
- the chip counts and the open-only toggle respect it;
- filter tests cover it, and it renders in light and dark themes. It depends on the core ticket.

Measure before fixing. The other tickets in E-7 take their numbers from this one.

**Done when:**
- a script (kept out of the default test run) builds synthetic stores of about 1k and 10k chunks with the real model, deterministically;
- it times: adding one document of N chunks, adding a batch, a keyword search, a hybrid search (split into process start, model map and query embedding), and a full index rebuild;
- the results are posted here and in each E-7 ticket they affect, including which costs are worth fixing.

At about 0.58 s per chunk, a 300-page PDF takes minutes to file and can exceed an agent's `kb_add` timeout.

**Done when:**
- a filed document is searchable **by keyword immediately**, and its vectors are filled in afterwards;
- search stays correct while vectors are missing: hybrid search uses what exists and says how many chunks are not embedded yet (no silent gaps);
- there is a way to finish the pending embeddings (a command, and the Knowledge UI shows progress);
- `kb_add` returns within an agent's tool timeout for large documents;
- tests cover the pending state, and the numbers come from the benchmark ticket.

`layout()` in `index-vscode/src/view/graph.ts` compares every pair of nodes, 300 times, on the webview's main thread. That is fine for a few hundred linked documents; with thousands the tab freezes.

**Done when:**
- the layout uses an approximation (e.g. Barnes–Hut) above a size threshold, and still gives the same picture for the same store;
- it runs off the main thread (or in time slices), so the tab stays responsive while it settles;
- the positions are cached per store state, so reopening the tab is instant;
- a test times 2,000 nodes against a budget, and the existing layout tests still pass.

Every `kb search` is a new process that maps the model and embeds the query, and every add rebuilds the keyword index from the whole store. Neither has been measured at scale.

**Done when** the benchmark ticket's numbers have been read and one of these is decided and, if warranted, built:
- **searches:** a query-embedding cache, or a long-lived process the MCP server and extension reuse. Only if a hybrid search on a warm file cache costs enough to matter when an agent runs a dozen in a row;
- **adds:** an incremental keyword index. Only if a rebuild at 10k chunks is slow enough to notice;
- otherwise the numbers are recorded here and the ticket is closed as not needed.

